<article class="blog-post">
  <div class="blog-post__kicker">Rehabilitation &nbsp;·&nbsp; Winter Care</div>
  <h1 class="blog-post__title">Winter in rehab: why some turtles stay with us until spring</h1>
  <p class="blog-post__subtitle">
    Every fall, the same question comes up: when do the turtles go home? For many of our
    patients, the answer is spring.
  </p>
  <div class="blog-post__meta">
    <span>Linda Nichols</span>
    <div class="blog-post__meta-dot"></div>
    <span>November 9, 2026</span>
    <div class="blog-post__meta-dot"></div>
    <span>Rehabilitation</span>
  </div>
  <div class="blog-post__body">
    <img src="/images/mira-profile.jpg" alt="Mira, an eastern box turtle in rehabilitation, in profile with a bright, open eye" width="1600" height="1200" />
    <p class="img-caption">Mira, one of the eastern box turtles spending this winter with us.</p>

    <p>
      Once Virginia nights turn cold, a turtle that is still healing cannot safely go back
      outside. So our current patients, and the new ones still coming in every week, stay with
      us through the winter.
    </p>

    <h2>What box turtles do in winter</h2>
    <p>
      Eastern box turtles (<em>Terrapene carolina carolina</em>) overwinter in shallow burrows
      called hibernacula. As fall temperatures drop, they dig into loose soil and leaf litter and
      stay there until spring. This period of dormancy in reptiles is called brumation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Box turtles do not dig deep. Most settle only a few inches down, where their body
      temperature hovers just above freezing for the rest of the winter. They stay there until
      soil temperatures rise in spring, which can mean several months underground.
    </p>
    <p>
      Shallow burrows mean box turtles can freeze, and remarkably, they can survive it. Adults
      have tolerated more than half of their body water freezing for up to three days without
      injury, making them the largest animals known to be naturally freeze tolerant. That
      tolerance has limits, though. Prolonged freezing does kill turtles in the wild.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many box turtles also return to the same overwintering areas year after year. Research here
      in Virginia suggests juveniles may pick poorer sites than adults in their first winters,
      which leaves them more exposed to the cold.
    </p>

    <h2>Why our patients don't brumate</h2>
    <p>
      Turtles are ectotherms. Their metabolism, immune response, and healing all depend on
      environmental temperature.
    </p>
    <p>
      In reptiles, both immune function and the inflammatory response depend on temperature, and
      cooler conditions slow healing. For most turtles, 79 to 90°F (26 to 32°C) is considered
      the right range for recovery.
    </p>
    <p>
      A brumating box turtle sits just above freezing for months. At that temperature, shell
      fractures stop knitting, wounds stop closing, and the immune system cannot effectively
      fight infection. For head-start hatchlings, brumation also pauses growth, which is the main
      goal of their time in care with us.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not let our patients brumate while they are in care. They stay warm, fed, and active
      through the winter so healing and growth continue.
    </p>

    <h2>What winter care looks like</h2>
    <p>
      Our turtles spend the winter in indoor areas where the ambient temperature stays between
      80 and 82 degrees, with the right humidity for each species. Each setup has UVB lighting,
      plus basking lights where they are needed.
    </p>
    <p>
      What a turtle lives in depends on why it is here. Patients recovering from injury or
      illness may be in a hospital setup on pads or newspaper, which is simpler and easier to
      keep clean while wounds heal. Eastern box turtle hatchlings and juveniles live on substrate
      they can dig into. Our head-start hatchlings move up to larger bins as they grow.
    </p>
    <p>
      All winter, while it's too cold for the turtles to get outdoor time, bulbs burn out,
      filters need replacing, and substrate gets changed out constantly. Multiply that by every
      turtle in care, for months, and winter is our most supply-heavy season.
    </p>

    <h2>Meet Mira</h2>
    <p>
      Mira is an eastern box turtle who came to us back in May. One eye was sealed shut. The
      other had a large tumor. She was also missing part of her jaw.
    </p>
    <img src="/images/mira-front.jpg" alt="Mira the eastern box turtle looking toward the camera with both eyes open" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" />
    <p class="img-caption">Mira today, with both eyes open.</p>
    <p>
      Today she can see out of both eyes and eats on her own. She has come a long way, but she is
      not ready to go back out before the cold sets in.
    </p>
    <p>
      So Mira is wintering with us. Warm, fed, and under UVB, her body can keep doing the work of
      healing instead of shutting down for months. We are optimistic she will be able to go home
      in the future. She seems to think so too!
    </p>

    <h2>How you can help</h2>
    <p>
      Keeping Mira and every other winter patient going takes lighting, water filtration, and
      substrate, and a lot of it. If you would like to help, our Amazon and Chewy wishlists
      have exactly what we use. You can find them on our <a href="/donate">donate page</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every item goes straight to the turtles. Come spring, we hope to be posting a lot of
      release videos.
    </p>

